<h1 id="colors">Colors and palettes</h1>
<p>Choose colors by their role in your design. Text and background utilities are separate, so one palette can change the appearance of the same markup.</p>
<h2 id="color-utilities">Text and backgrounds</h2>
<p>Compare the primary and secondary text/background pairs side by side. Text color and background color each need their own class.</p>
{% set markup %}<div class="grid w-100" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr))">
  <div class="py-40 flex items-center justify-center text-center fs-14 lh-20 clr-primary clr-primary-bg">
    <div>clr-primary<br>clr-primary-bg</div>
  </div>
  <div class="py-40 flex items-center justify-center text-center fs-14 lh-20 clr-secondary clr-secondary-bg">
    <div>clr-secondary<br>clr-secondary-bg</div>
  </div>
</div>{% endset %}
{{ example(markup, "Primary and secondary color pairs") }}
<p>Two equal grid tracks share the available width. This example defines its always-on track pattern inline instead of adding another shared utility. Vertical padding supplies the box height while the flex utilities center the labels.</p>
<h2 id="buttons">Buttons</h2>
<p>Use <code>btn</code> for an inline-flex button with inherited typography, padding, a border, rounded corners, and a visible keyboard focus outline. Use <code>btn btn-primary</code> for a solid palette fill with white text and modest rounded corners. Use <code>btn btn-secondary</code> for a transparent pill with matching colored text and outline. The base button inherits text color and has a transparent background, so it can also be used inside a colored region.</p>
{% set markup %}<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-16 items-center">
  <button class="btn" type="button">btn</button>
  <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button">btn btn-primary</button>
  <button class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">btn btn-secondary</button>
  <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" disabled>Unavailable action</button>
</div>{% endset %}
{{ example(markup, "Primary and secondary buttons") }}
<p>Add <code>btn-sm</code> to either appearance variant for 12px text, 18px line-height, and 6px vertical / 12px horizontal padding. Size and appearance are independent. Without <code>btn-sm</code>, buttons retain inherited typography and default <code>0.75rem 1rem</code> padding. See the <a href="/ui/buttons/">small primary and secondary examples</a>.</p>
<p>These sample buttons demonstrate appearance; they do not run an action. Use a native <code>button</code> for actions and an anchor with a real destination for navigation. Native <code>disabled</code> prevents the unavailable button from activating; it also receives a dimmed appearance. Do not add <code>disabled</code> to an anchor.</p>
<p>Use existing spacing and layout utilities for variations, such as <code>btn btn-primary py-8 px-16</code>. Primary fills use the existing deeper light-mode primary accent in both modes through <code>--btn-primary-bg</code>; single-palette consumers use <code>$clr-primary</code>. Labels use fixed white. Secondary text and outlines use the current mode’s secondary accent. Palette overrides flow through both variants. Check text, borders, focus outlines, and disabled states against their actual surroundings when customizing colors; the named pairs do not guarantee accessible contrast for every palette.</p>
<p>Primary and secondary are persistent visual variants, not selected and unselected states. Keep the same variant class when state changes. A held press (<code>:active</code>), <code>aria-pressed="true"</code>, <code>aria-selected="true"</code>, or <code>aria-expanded="true"</code> slightly darkens the same primary fill or adds a stronger tinted fill inside the secondary outline. Primary keeps white text and modest rounded corners. Secondary stays pill-shaped with matching text and outline: its idle background is transparent, hover adds a subtle palette tint, and selected or expanded states use a stronger tint. Use the ARIA attribute appropriate to the control: pressed for toggle buttons, selected for tabs, and expanded for disclosures.</p>
